query:
Misc. bug: chat-template rendering is O(N²) in emitted parts (gather_string_parts)

### Name and Version

version: 10252 (fe2adf0)
built with Clang 21.1.8 for Linux x86_64

### Operating systems

Linux

### Which llama.cpp modules do you know to be affected?

llama-server

### Command line

```shell
// Plese check the problem description

cmake -B build-clean -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
      -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF \
      -DLLAMA_BUILD_SERVER=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF
cmake --build build-clean --target llama-common -j
```

### Problem description & steps to reproduce


Note: The defect is in common/jinja/ (gather_string_parts), which is shared by all tools that render chat templates. **llama-server is the one where the collection sizes come from the request, so it is the only one where it is reachable at meaningful scale.**

# `gather_string_parts` merges string parts in O(N²); chat-template rendering collapses as tool count grows

### Summary

`jinja::runtime::gather_string_parts` (`common/jinja/runtime.h`) merges adjacent string parts by calling `vector::erase` inside a loop. Each erase shifts every following element, so an O(N) merge costs O(N²). Under callgrind this one loop accounts for **57% of all instructions** executed during a chat-template render.

The visible effect is that render time explodes with the size of the collections a template
iterates — most commonly `tools`. With the bundled
`CohereForAI-c4ai-command-r-plus-tool_use.jinja`, unmodified:

| tools | properties per tool | seconds | with the patch below |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 5 | 5 | 0.031 | — |
| 20 | 10 | 0.177 | — |
| 20 | 20 | 0.434 | **0.222** |
| 40 | 30 | 2.425 | 0.649 |
| 50 | 50 | 9.295 | **1.453** |
| 100 | 100 | 128.5 | **6.923** |

20–40 tools is an ordinary agentic setup, and it already costs half a second to two seconds of CPU per request before a single token is generated. The larger sizes are included to show where the curve goes, not because anyone sends them.

The patch produces **byte-identical prompts** at every size tested (26,521 / 149,971 / 587,721 bytes).

### The cause

```cpp
// common/jinja/runtime.h
static value_string gather_string_parts(const value & val) {
    value_string parts = mk_val<value_string>();
    gather_string_parts_recursive(val, parts);
    // join consecutive parts with the same type
    auto & p = parts->val_str.parts;
    for (size_t i = 1; i < p.size(); ) {
        if (p[i].is_input == p[i - 1].is_input) {
            p[i - 1].val += p[i].val;
            p.erase(p.begin() + i);      // <-- O(N) shift, inside the loop
        } else {
            i++;
        }
    }
    return parts;
}
```

`jinja::string` is a `std::vector<string_part>`. A render that emits N pieces produces N parts, nearly all carrying the same `is_input` flag, so nearly every iteration erases, each erase memmoves the tail. N merges cost O(N²) element moves.

callgrind, 20 tools × 20 properties:

```
54.31%  < caller: jinja::runtime::gather_string_parts        (15,460 calls)
36.39%    std::vector<jinja::string_part>::_M_erase          (35,121,435 calls)
20.84%    __memcpy_avx_unaligned_erms                        (the shifting)
```

`gather_string_parts` is also called 15,460 times for this one render, each call re-running the merge over the parts accumulated so far.

### Suggested patch

A single in-place compaction pass, O(N):

```cpp
// join consecutive parts with the same type (single in-place pass)
auto & p = parts->val_str.parts;
if (!p.empty()) {
    size_t w = 0;
    for (size_t r = 1; r < p.size(); r++) {
        if (p[w].is_input == p[r].is_input) {
            p[w].val += p[r].val;
        } else if (++w != r) {
            p[w] = std::move(p[r]);
        }
    }
    p.resize(w + 1);
}
```

The `++w != r` guard matters: without it the move degenerates into self-move-assignment whenever no merge has happened yet, and a self-moved `std::string` is left in an unspecified state. 
Our first attempt omitted it and silently lost 39 bytes of prompt, caught only because the reproduction below prints the rendered length.

The project's own tests pass with it applied: **`test-jinja`, `test-chat-template`, `test-chat-auto-parser` and `test-chat-peg-parser` all pass**. (`test-chat` does not build in this configuration — it needs `mtmd.h`, which `-DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF` excludes — and fails the same way without the patch, so it is unrelated.)

### Reproduction

Needs no model weights and no GPU.

```bash
cmake -B build-clean -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -DCMAKE_C_COMPILER=clang -DCMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=clang++ \
      -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF \
      -DLLAMA_BUILD_SERVER=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TOOLS=OFF
cmake --build build-clean --target llama-common -j
# note: the target is `llama-common`; `--target common` exits 0 while building nothing

cat > poc.cpp <<'EOF'
// Cost of common_chat_templates_apply as a function of collection sizes, using a template
// shipped in models/templates/ unmodified.
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdio>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

#include <nlohmann/json.hpp>
#include "chat.h"

using json = nlohmann::ordered_json;

static std::string slurp(const char * path) {
    std::ifstream f(path);
    std::stringstream ss;
    ss << f.rdbuf();
    return ss.str();
}

static double render(common_chat_templates * tmpls, int n_tools, int n_props,
                     size_t * out_bytes, const char ** out_status) {
    common_chat_templates_inputs in;
    common_chat_msg msg;
    msg.role    = "user";
    msg.content = "hi";
    in.messages              = {msg};
    in.use_jinja             = true;
    in.add_generation_prompt = true;

    for (int i = 0; i < n_tools; i++) {
        json props = json::object();
        for (int p = 0; p < n_props; p++) {
            props["p" + std::to_string(p)] = {{"type", "string"}, {"description", "d"}};
        }
        common_chat_tool tool;
        tool.name        = "t" + std::to_string(i);
        tool.description = "d";
        tool.parameters  = json{{"type", "object"}, {"properties", props}}.dump();
        in.tools.push_back(tool);
    }

    const auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
    *out_bytes  = 0;
    *out_status = "ok";
    try {
        *out_bytes = common_chat_templates_apply(tmpls, in).prompt.size();
    } catch (const std::exception & e) {
        *out_status = e.what();
    } catch (...) {
        *out_status = "unknown exception";
    }
    return std::chrono::duration<double>(std::chrono::steady_clock::now() - t0).count();
}

int main(int argc, char ** argv) {
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("usage: poc <template.jinja>\n");
        return 2;
    }
    auto tmpls = common_chat_templates_init(nullptr, slurp(argv[1]));
    if (!tmpls) {
        printf("template init failed\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("%7s %7s %10s  %s\n", "tools", "props", "seconds", "render");
    for (auto [nt, np] : {std::pair<int,int>{5,5}, {20,10}, {20,20},
                          {40,30}, {50,50}, {100,100}}) {
        size_t bytes = 0;
        const char * status = "";
        const double dt = render(tmpls.get(), nt, np, &bytes, &status);
        // the outcome sits next to the timing, so the number cannot be mistaken for the
        // cost of throwing rather than the cost of rendering
        printf("%7d %7d %10.3f  [%s, prompt %zu bytes]\n", nt, np, dt, status, bytes);
        fflush(stdout);
    }
    return 0;
}
EOF

clang++ -std=c++17 -O2 -Icommon -Iinclude -Iggml/include -Ivendor poc.cpp \
    -Lbuild-clean/bin -lllama-common -lllama -lggml -lggml-base \
    -Wl,-rpath,build-clean/bin -o poc

# the rpath is relative, so run from the repository root
./poc models/templates/CohereForAI-c4ai-command-r-plus-tool_use.jinja
```

Not specific to one template: `ByteDance-Seed-OSS.jinja` gives 92 s at 100 × 100 unpatched. **55 of the 64 templates in `models/templates/` contain nested `for` loops**, and in most of them both levels iterate caller-supplied collections — `for tool in tools` over `for param in tool.function.parameters.properties`.

### The cost tracks the number of emissions, not the number of bytes

Three controls, using `common_chat_verify_template` with `{% for i in range(N) %}<pad>{% endfor %}`:

**A — total output pinned at 256 KB, emission count varied 64×**

| emissions | bytes each | seconds |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 256 | 1024 | 0.116 |
| 1,024 | 256 | 0.763 |
| 4,096 | 64 | 9.544 |
| 16,384 | 16 | **141.5** |

**B — emissions pinned at 2,048, total output varied 16×**

| emissions | bytes each | seconds |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 2,048 | 32 | 2.461 |
| 2,048 | 512 | 2.873 |

**C — an empty loop body is exactly linear**

| iterations | empty body | emitting body |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 2,048 | 0.39 | 2.64 |
| 4,096 | 0.78 | 9.95 |
| 40,000 | 7.57 | — |

Producing the same 256 KB costs 0.116 s or 141.5 s depending only on how many pieces it was assembled from, 64× the emissions for 1,220× the time, while 16× the bytes at a fixed emission count costs 1.17×. The empty loop doubles exactly with N, so the iteration machinery is linear and the super-linear term is in the merge.

**What this does not say.** Cost is not a function of `tools × properties` alone: at a fixed product of 2,500 the shapes differ by 4.5× (50 × 50: 9.23 s; 1 × 2,500: 41.57 s) at the same output size, so the shape matters too. The claim here is only that the merge is quadratic in the part count.

### A separate, smaller observation

Control C also shows 40,000 iterations of an **empty** `{% for %}{% endfor %}` costing 7.57 s. That is linear, so it is not the subject
of this report, but the constant looks high and it is independent of the merge.


### Environment

llama.cpp at `fe2adf0`, `Release`, clang 21, no sanitizers, x86-64. Timings can totally vary by machine; the growth is the claim.


