This is affecting me, too. I'm surprised it hasn't hit more people yet since a lot of videos are like this. @ngxson The reason is that ffmpeg needs a seekable stream to read these files, and the fix is to write the video to a temporary file somewhere and point ffmpeg to the file rather than piping it.

I made this change (writing to the filesystem) locally in llama.cpp and can confirm that these videos now process successfully. Prior to the change, as reported there would be a silent failure for any videos that qualified and the model wouldn't see them.

I think there are ways you can write to an in-memory file descriptor only visible to the process and (ffmpeg) child if you are trying to preserve the "in-memory only" property as much as possible.

