query:
Eval bug: macOS arm64: heap corruption due to ggml-cpu precompiled header

[cpp-pch-on.log](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/32165134/cpp-pch-on.log)

### Name and Version

 b10919, built from source. Compiler AppleClang 21.0.0.21000101. Last good: b10909

### Operating systems

Mac

### GGML backends

Metal, CPU

### Hardware

Discovered running on GH macOS runner CPU
```
GitHub macos-26-arm64 runner, image 20260907.0351.1
```

### Models

SmolLM-135M.Q2_K.gguf (https://huggingface.co/QuantFactory/SmolLM-135M-GGUF)

### Problem description & steps to reproduce

The commit that added the `target_precompile_headers` recently for macOS had an unexpected side effect resulting in heap corruption. Eventually, it causes a `SIGTRAP` due to an out of bounds memory write. Since the actual crash occurs later on, it was extermemly hard to pin down. In my initial testing, the allocation that triggered it was usually the lazy compile of the Metal rope pipeline, but it turned out not to be the cause.

Eventually after many test runs, I was able to track it down to the pre-compiled headers change. I did a bunch of test runs both with and without to be certain.

| Build | cmake_pch files, ggml / all | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Unchanged | 4 / 16 | stops on attempt 1, 20 of 20 decode failures |
| `-DCMAKE_DISABLE_PRECOMPILE_HEADERS=ON` | 0 / 0 | no stop, 0 failures |
| The one line removed | 0 / 12 | no stop, 0 failures |

Since our project code is in Go using the ffi interface, I needed to be certain it was not something related to our own code. Hence, I created a minimal reproducer in C++:
```c++
// Create a context with the library defaults, tokenizes two tokens and call
// llama_decode one time. There is no warmup and no graph reuse.
#include "llama.h"

#include <cstdio>
#include <cstring>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

int main(int argc, char ** argv) {
    std::string model_file;
    std::string mode = "getone";

    for (int i = 1; i < argc; i++) {
        if (strcmp(argv[i], "-model") == 0 && i + 1 < argc) {
            model_file = argv[++i];
        } else if (strcmp(argv[i], "-mode") == 0 && i + 1 < argc) {
            mode = argv[++i];
        }
    }

    if (model_file.empty()) {
        fprintf(stderr, "error: -model is necessary\n");
        return 1;
    }

    llama_backend_init();

    llama_model * model = llama_model_load_from_file(model_file.c_str(), llama_model_default_params());
    if (model == nullptr) {
        fprintf(stderr, "error: unable to load model from file %s\n", model_file.c_str());
        return 1;
    }

    llama_context * ctx = llama_init_from_model(model, llama_context_default_params());
    if (ctx == nullptr) {
        fprintf(stderr, "error: unable to initialize context from model\n");
        return 1;
    }

    const llama_vocab * vocab = llama_model_get_vocab(model);

    const char * text = "Hello world";
    std::vector<llama_token> tokens(64);
    const int32_t n = llama_tokenize(vocab, text, strlen(text), tokens.data(), (int32_t) tokens.size(), true, true);
    if (n < 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "error: unable to tokenize\n");
        return 1;
    }
    tokens.resize(n);

    llama_batch batch;
    if (mode == "getone") {
        batch = llama_batch_get_one(tokens.data(), n);
    } else if (mode == "full") {
        // llama_batch_init leaves the members uninitialized, so every one of
        // them gets a value, which is what the Go Add does.
        batch = llama_batch_init(n, 0, 1);
        batch.n_tokens = n;
        for (int32_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            batch.token[i]     = tokens[i];
            batch.pos[i]       = i;
            batch.n_seq_id[i]  = 1;
            batch.seq_id[i][0] = 0;
            batch.logits[i]    = i == n - 1;
        }
    } else {
        fprintf(stderr, "error: unknown mode %s\n", mode.c_str());
        return 1;
    }

    const int32_t ret = llama_decode(ctx, batch);
    if (ret != 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "error: decode returned %d\n", ret);
        return 1;
    }

    printf("mode %s decoded %d tokens\n", mode.c_str(), n);

    if (mode == "full") {
        llama_batch_free(batch);
    }
    llama_free(ctx);
    llama_model_free(model);
    llama_backend_free();

    return 0;
}
```

Here is how to run it:
```
cmake -B build -DGGML_METAL_EMBED_LIBRARY=ON -DCMAKE_OSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=13.3 \
  -DCMAKE_INSTALL_RPATH='@loader_path' -DCMAKE_BUILD_WITH_INSTALL_RPATH=ON \
  -DLLAMA_BUILD_SERVER=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_APP=OFF -DLLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DLLAMA_CURL=OFF
cmake --build build --config Release -j $(sysctl -n hw.logicalcpu)

clang++ -std=c++17 -O2 -o decode main.cpp -Iinclude -Iggml/include \
  -Lbuild/bin -lllama -Wl,-rpath,build/bin

lldb -b -o "settings set target.env-vars DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES=/usr/lib/libgmalloc.dylib" \
  -o run -k 'bt all' -- ./decode -mode full -model SmolLM-135M.Q2_K.gguf
```

Adding `-DCMAKE_DISABLE_PRECOMPILE_HEADERS=ON` to the cmake line, or deleting the one `target_precompile_headers` line, makes the stop go away.i

